},{"season":2023,"team":"TB","distance":0.5541,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Todd Bowles"},{"season":2019,"team":"NYG","distance":0.6123,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Panthers went 2-15 in 2023 and were outscored 416 to 236. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 30th in EPA per play and the defense 27th in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the 2nd lowest play-action rate in the league.","offense":"The Panthers offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd lowest play-action rate and the 2nd most receivers on the field in the league. Bryce Young ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Adam Thielen, who drew 26% of the targets.","defense":"The Panthers defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the pass than the run, ranking 16th and 32nd. Brian Burns led the way with 8 sacks. Their scheme was fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature was a base defense usage that ranked 5th highest in the league.","special":"The Panthers were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 48% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 83% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 24% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 31% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Bryce Young played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,877 yards. Adam Thielen was the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 1,014 receiving yards. Chuba Hubbard handled most of the work in the backfield with 59% of the carries. On defense, Brian Burns led the team with 8 sacks and Xavier Woods picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Frank Reich called the offense as head coach and Ejiro Evero called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2022 Colts under Frank Reich and the 2025 Saints under Kellen Moore. Their defense looked most like the 2024 Giants and the 2024 Steelers.","trends":"The offense went from 20th in EPA per play in 2022 to 30th in 2023, a big step back. The defense moved from 16th in EPA allowed per play in 2022 to 27th in 2023, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was screen rate, which went from 12% to 7%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2023. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}